{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbcriFQQV5MpCjMDVdSm", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Releases are cut by pushing a git tag in the form `vYYYY.MM.N`. CI then builds and publishes the release. Don't publish by hand.\n\n```sh\ngit checkout main && git pull\ngit tag v2026.10.1        # Nth release this month; today is 2026-10-01\ngit push origin v2026.10.1\n```\n\nThat process comes from a team note in the project's memory store. I couldn't confirm it from the repo itself:\n\n- **No CI config in the repo.** There's no `.github/workflows` or similar, so I can't see the pipeline that runs on a tag push. It's presumably configured somewhere outside this repo.\n- **No tags exist locally.** I can't check how past tags were named. The fetch for that step was skipped, so remote tags may exist; `git fetch --tags` will show them.\n- **The version number is also hardcoded.** `pyproject.toml` has `version = \"2026.9.1\"`, which matches the tag scheme without the leading zero or `v`. Nothing documents whether you should bump it before tagging or whether CI sets it from the tag. If CI doesn't set it, bump it to `2026.10.1` and commit before you tag, so the published package version matches the tag.\n- **Run the tests first.** Run `make test`, which is the full suite including the Postgres tests, not just `make test-fast`.\n\nIf someone on the team knows where the release CI lives and how the `pyproject.toml` version is handled, I can add that to the memory note."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 502, "cache_read_input_tokens": 25820, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 502}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "